      const baseLayer = new XRWebGLLayer(session, navigator.gl);
      session.updateRenderState({ baseLayer });
      const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({
        canvas: {
          // mock the canvas
          addEventListener() { },
        },
        context: navigator.gl,
      });
      renderer.xr.enabled = true;
      renderer.xr.setReferenceSpaceType('local');
      renderer.xr.setSession(session);
      function animate() {
        obj.rotation.x += 0.01;
        obj.rotation.y += 0.01;
        renderer.render(scene, camera);
      }
      camera.position.z = 5;
      renderer.setAnimationLoop(animate);
    }, (err) => {
      console.warn('Failed to start XR session:', err);
    });
  </script>
</head>
<body>
</body>
</html>
Now let's explain the code above one by one.

data.create-three-renderer#Create three rende:
At traditional Web browser, to get 3d library to work, developer needs to create a canvas to get the WebGL context, then create library's renderer to start rendering.
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer();
The above line would call document.createElement('canvas') to create a canvas. Developer is able to pass a canvas element to the constructor to use it as the rendering canvas.
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ canvas: document.getElementById('custom-canvas') });
In traditional Web browser, the render target is the browser window, but in JSAR, all applications (including WebXR and host) are sharing the same render target, that means the WebGL context is also shared. To create a three renderer:
const gl = navigator.gl;
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({
  canvas: {
    // mock the canvas
    addEventListener() { },
  },
  context: gl,
});
JSAR runtime exposes a new readonly property gl: WebGL2RenderingContext on the navigator object, application could use this property to make draw calls on the shared context.

data.request-an-xr-session#Request an XR sess:
In JSAR, developer doesn't need a AR/VR button to trigger the XR session, developer could request the session directly because the device is already in XR mode:
navigator.xr.requestSession('immersive-ar', {}).then((session) => {
  const baseLayer = new XRWebGLLayer(session, navigator.gl);
  session.updateRenderState({ baseLayer });
  renderer.xr.enabled = true;
  renderer.xr.setReferenceSpaceType('local');
  renderer.xr.setSession(session);
});
The above code is how to configure the three.js library to work with WebXR, the same code could be used in traditional Web browser.

data.start-the-animation-loop#Start the anima:
Now both the three renderer and WebXR configuration are ready, the last step is to start the animation loop:
function animate() {
  obj.rotation.x += 0.01;
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}
renderer.setAnimationLoop(animate);
Three.js renderer.setAnimationLoop method is used to start the animation loop, internally it will call the XRSession's requestAnimationFrame method to get the next frame callback and render the scene at that time.

data.trembedder-class#TrEmbedder class:
TrEmbedder is the main virtual class that should be inherited by the embedding application, it contains the main functions that should be implemented by the embedding application.
class SimpleEmbedder : public TrEmbedder
{
public:
  DesktopEmbedder() : TrEmbedder()
  {
    auto renderer = constellation->getRenderer();
    auto api = RenderAPI::Create(kUnityGfxRendererOpenGLCore, getConstellation());
    renderer->setApi(api);
  }
};

data.constructor#Constructor:
The TrEmbedder constructor signature is:
TrEmbedder(TrHostEngine hostEngine = TrHostEngine::None)
There is a TrHostEngine enum that embedding developers could use to specify the host engine that they are using, the default value is TrHostEngine::None, which means the embedding application is a standalone.The possible values for TrHostEngine are:
enum class TrHostEngine
{
  None,   // Standalone
  Unity,  // Embedding in Unity
  Unreal, // Embedding in Unreal
  Cocos,  // Embedding in Cocos
};

data.trembedder#~TrEmbedder():
If the custom embedder class has its own resources, the developers should release them in a custom destructor.
~TrEmbedder() {
  // Release resources
}

data.bool-oneventtrevent-event-trcontentrunti:
This virtual method will be called by the runtime when there is an event to be handled by the embedding application.
bool onEvent(TrEvent &event, TrContentRuntime *content) {
  // Handle the event
  return true;
}

data.void-shutdown#void shutdown():
This method should be called to shutdown the runtime, it will release all the resources and stop the internal components, it will be returned when all the resources are released.
embedder->shutdown();
// Wait until the runtime is shutdown

